1. What does the application do?

Palette studio is a sharp, modern interactive color curation tool
built to streamline the brand layout generation process. The
application enables users to instantly compile sets of five
harmonized cards by clicking the prominent "Generate new palette"
button or pressing the Spacebar keyboard command shortcut.

Every color block displays its unique uppercase HEX code string
clearly along the baseline. Users can click directly on any color
card to copy the hex code immediately to their system clipboard.
To protect high-performing design combinations, each swatch
contains a hover-state shortcut menu featuring a Lock function 
to freeze the color in place during subsequent palette rolls, 
alongside a dedicated Copy button for swift extracting.

Additionally, the interface tracks user bookmarks in real time with
an integrated "Saved / Favorites" badge counter layout in the main
toolbar.
Note: The "Saved" feature directly saves the palette to your
device.


2. Who is the target audience it is built for?

The application is custom-built for UI/UX Designers(mainly), who 
require rapid, friction-free aesthetic ideation during initial
style-guide wireframing and prototyping stages.

It eliminates the clunky administrative process of manually editing
hex strings by providing an intuitive, hyper-responsive visual canvas.

3. What did you learn?

 A.  Event-Driven Keyboard Integration: I learned how to intercept global window keyboard event listeners natively in JavaScript (keydown tracking for the Space bar) to execute central UI generator algorithms without requiring continuous button clicks.

B   Component-Level Conditional State Management: The AI guided me on how to track individual Boolean array maps for the swatches (tracking whether isLocked is true or false). This ensures the generator engine selectively preserves targeted index blocks while updating unlocked values.

C.  Modern Glassmorphic Dark-UI Aesthetics: I learned how to build high-end dark interfaces using modern CSS layer techniques—combining backdrop blurs (backdrop-filter), absolute overlay spacing rules for contextual utility menus, and glowing radial border shadows for principal action targets.

